---
import LessonLayout   from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader   from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonCallout  from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonDiagram  from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
---

<LessonLayout title="2.3 — Weights, Embeddings & Attention: A Room Full of People">

  <LessonHeader
    num="2.3"
    title="Weights, Embeddings & Attention: A Room Full of People"
    subtitle="One scene, no math yet — everything from here builds on this exact picture."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <p>
      Before any formulas: one consistent real-world scene, and every core idea — weights,
      embeddings, attention, feedforward, layers — mapped onto it directly. The next lesson
      (2.4) covers the same ideas with real terminology and code. This one is just the picture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Scene</h2>

    <p>
      You're at a loud party. Someone taps your shoulder and asks:
      <strong>"What did Sarah say about the trip?"</strong> You don't listen to everyone in the
      room equally — you unconsciously tune toward whoever's relevant and tune out the rest.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-room">
      <div class="l0008-you">
        <span class="l0008-avatar">🙋</span>
        <div class="l0008-bubble">"What did Sarah say about the trip?"</div>
      </div>
      <div class="l0008-people">
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--high">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍‍♀️</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Sarah</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ trip · travel · delay</div>
        </div>
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Neighbor</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ sports · weekend</div>
        </div>
        <div class="l0008-person l0008-person--low">
          <span class="l0008-avatar">🧍‍♂️</span>
          <div class="l0008-name">Bartender</div>
          <div class="l0008-sign">🏷️ drinks · orders</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">Sarah's "sign" matches your question — she lights up. The others fade into background noise.</p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Embeddings — Meaning as Coordinates, Not Labels</h2>

    <p>
      For that "who's relevant" instinct to become something a computer can actually compute,
      everyone in the room first needs a precise <strong>coordinate</strong> — not a word tag,
      an actual point in space, decided by meaning. People and topics that are similar end up
      near each other on this map.
    </p>

    <div class="l0008-map">
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:64%;top:20%;" data-label="Sarah"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:58%;top:32%;" data-label="trip"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:70%;top:38%;" data-label="vacation"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot l0008-dot--person" style="left:20%;top:58%;" data-label="Bartender"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:16%;top:74%;" data-label="drinks"></span>
      <span class="l0008-dot" style="left:30%;top:80%;" data-label="spreadsheet"></span>
    </div>
    <p class="l0008-caption">"Trip" and "vacation" land close together. "Spreadsheet" lands far from both — different meaning, different neighborhood.</p>

    <p>
      Why coordinates specifically, not just labels? Three concrete reasons:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>You can calculate a match score.</strong> Two labels ("trip" vs. "vacation")
        give a computer nothing to work with. Two coordinates let you do real arithmetic —
        multiply and add the numbers — and get back an actual match score you can rank people
        by, instead of a guess.</li>
      <li><strong>You can blend information.</strong> You can't "70%-blend" two sentences. You
        can take 70% of one coordinate and 30% of another, number by number, and get a new,
        meaningful coordinate back — this is exactly what happens once relevance is decided.</li>
      <li><strong>Relationships become measurable, not hand-written.</strong> Nobody tells the
        model "trip and vacation are similar" — words used in similar contexts naturally end up
        at similar coordinates as a side effect of training. Because it's numeric, that
        closeness is something you can measure, not something a human pre-labeled.</li>
      </ul>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Attention — Query, Key, Value</h2>

    <p>
      Back at the party, three things are happening at once:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Query</strong> — the question itself, in your head: <em>"who knows about the trip?"</em></li>
      <li><strong>Key</strong> — what each person is silently broadcasting about what they might know (their "sign").</li>
      <li><strong>Value</strong> — the actual information that person would give you if you turned to listen.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      You compare your query against everyone's key. High match → listen closely. Low match →
      mostly ignore. Then you blend together what everyone would tell you, weighted by how well
      their key matched — not "pick the one best person," a <em>blend</em>, weighted by
      relevance.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  Q["❓ Query&colon; what did Sarah<br/>say about the trip?"]:::query
  K1["🏷️ Sarah's key&colon;<br/>trip, travel, delay"]:::key
  K2["🏷️ Neighbor's key&colon;<br/>sports, weekend"]:::key
  K3["🏷️ Bartender's key&colon;<br/>drinks, orders"]:::key
  Q -->|"70% match"| K1
  Q -->|"5% match"| K2
  Q -->|"3% match"| K3
  K1 --> V1["💬 Sarah's value"]:::value
  K2 --> V2["💬 Neighbor's value"]:::value
  K3 --> V3["💬 Bartender's value"]:::value
  V1 -->|"× 0.70"| OUT["🧠 Blended answer&colon;<br/>mostly Sarah, a little of everyone else"]:::out
  V2 -->|"× 0.05"| OUT
  V3 -->|"× 0.03"| OUT

  classDef query fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef key    fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef value  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out    fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Weights — Where the Judgment Comes From</h2>

    <p>
      How do you even know how to read someone's "sign" and match it to your question? That
      skill isn't innate — years of conversations taught you what "relevant" sounds like.
      <strong>Weights are that trained judgment, stored as numbers.</strong> Before any
      experience, your judgment is just random guessing — you'd rate the bartender's chatter as
      highly as Sarah's, with no basis to tell them apart. Training is the slow process of
      correcting that judgment based on whether your guesses turned out right.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Untrained ≠ broken, just random">
      <p>
        An untrained model has this exact same wiring — query, key, value, blending — fully
        functional. What it doesn't have is good judgment. Its weights are random numbers, so
        its "relevance scores" are meaningless, and its blended answer is noise. The
        architecture works from the start; the <em>judgment</em> only exists after training.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Fixed Judgment, Changing Room</h2>

    <p>
      Your room-reading skill — how you interpret a "sign," how you judge relevance — was built
      over years of past experience, and it doesn't change during any given party. To actually
      change that underlying judgment, you'd have to go live more experiences and let them
      reshape you. That's retraining. Nothing about one evening updates your instincts.
    </p>

    <p>
      But <strong>who's currently in the room is a completely different thing</strong> — that's
      not your judgment, that's just what's in front of you right now. You don't need new life
      experience to notice someone walked in and remember what they said five minutes ago.
      You're using your already-fixed skill to process an unfolding scene. The skill is frozen;
      what it's currently looking at keeps changing.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Parametric memory vs. context">
      <p>
        This split has a name: <strong>parametric memory</strong> (facts baked into the weights
        via training — slow, expensive, needs retraining to change) versus <strong>context</strong>
        (whatever's currently in the room — cheap, instant, gone once the session ends). Walk
        into a new conversation tomorrow and the room is empty again — same trained skill, zero
        memory of last night, unless someone specifically hands you a transcript at the door.
        This is the entire idea behind RAG (retrieval-augmented generation): rather than
        retraining a model to "know" something new, you just make sure the relevant fact walks
        into the room as part of the context, and let the model's already-frozen judgment work
        with it.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Feedforward — The Private Thinking Step</h2>

    <p>
      After listening around the room, you don't immediately blurt out an answer — you take a
      beat, privately, to make sense of what you gathered. Nobody else is involved; it's just
      you, alone, processing. Attention is "who did I listen to." Feedforward is "what did I
      privately make of it." Every person in the room does their own private processing,
      independently.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Layers — Doing the Whole Cycle Again</h2>

    <p>
      One round of "listen around the room, then think privately" gives a rough first
      impression. Doing it again — with your now-slightly-better understanding — refines it
      further.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  L1["Layer 1&colon;<br/>'they're talking<br/>about a trip'"]:::step --> L2["Layer 2&colon;<br/>'Sarah seems<br/>annoyed about it'"]:::step --> L3["Layer 3&colon;<br/>'annoyed because<br/>of a delayed flight'"]:::done

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      Each layer is one more round of that same two-step cycle (listen, then think privately),
      each one building on the last.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The Room Isn't Static</h2>

    <p>
      Two more things worth being precise about, since the scene so far made the room sound
      like a snapshot.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>People keep entering.</strong> Every new token is a new person walking through the
      door. Once someone's in the room, they stay — but a person who just walked in can listen
      to everyone already present, while nobody already present ever turns around to reconsider
      what a newcomer just said. Their read on the conversation was formed before the newcomer
      arrived, and it's locked in. That rule is called <strong>causal masking</strong>, and it's
      why generation has to happen one token at a time, in order — nobody in the room is allowed
      to know who's about to walk in next. The room also has a capacity — the
      <strong>context window</strong> — and letting people in forever eventually means either
      turning people away at the door or the room getting too crowded to track everything in it
      well.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The same name can mean different people.</strong> Imagine two different Sarahs at
      the party. Glance at name tags alone and they're identical — that name tag is the
      <strong>static embedding</strong>, the same starting coordinate every time, regardless of
      which Sarah or which sentence. But as each Sarah actually participates — stands near
      different people, gets folded into different conversations — her representation gets
      refined, layer after layer, by who's around her. By the third layer, "Sarah near the
      kitchen talking about flights" and "Sarah by the window talking about her thesis" have
      drifted into genuinely different coordinates, despite starting at the exact same spot.
      That's a <strong>contextual embedding</strong> — the same word reshaped differently every
      time by whatever surrounds it, which is a big part of why this architecture beats older
      one-fixed-vector-per-word methods.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Beam — Not Committing Too Early</h2>

    <p>
      If it's unclear yet whether the topic is "the trip" or "the wedding" — both got
      mentioned — instead of committing to one guess immediately, keep both possibilities in
      mind as you keep listening, and let more information settle which one actually pans out.
      Lesson 2.5 walks through this live, on the running <code>17 × 24</code> example.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="lesson-content">
    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Go Further</h2>
    <p>
      For real, interactive versions of these ideas running against actual trained models — not
      this lesson's illustrative scene — see the collection at
      <a href="https://dentro.de/ai/visualizations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dentro.de/ai/visualizations</a>,
      in particular a live GPT-2 walkthrough with real attention visualization and a step-by-step
      breakdown of real text generation.
    </p>
  </div>

</LessonLayout>

<style>
  .l0008-caption {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* ── The Room ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .l0008-room {
    background: var(--bg2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  .l0008-you {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .l0008-bubble {
    background: var(--accent-lt, var(--bg3));
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--ink);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.85rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .l0008-people {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .l0008-person {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    min-width: 8rem;
    transition: opacity .2s, border-color .2s, transform .2s;
  }
  .l0008-person--high {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px var(--accent-lt, rgba(37,99,235,.15));
    transform: scale(1.04);
  }
  .l0008-person--low { opacity: 0.55; }
  .l0008-avatar { font-size: 1.6rem; line-height: 1; }
  .l0008-name { font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.35rem; }
  .l0008-sign {
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }

  /* ── Map of Meaning ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .l0008-map {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    background: var(--bg2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .l0008-dot {
    position: absolute;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    margin-left: -5px;
    margin-top: -5px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--muted);
  }
  .l0008-dot--person { background: var(--accent); width: 12px; height: 12px; margin-left: -6px; margin-top: -6px; }
  .l0008-dot::after {
    content: attr(data-label);
    position: absolute;
    top: 14px;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem;
  }
</style>
